WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.474
This video lays the foundation of USB: speeds, endpoints, the four transfer types, descriptors, and the bandwidth math that decides your camera formats.

00:00:09.874 --> 00:00:16.015
Endpoint: A numbered mailbox on a USB device; each data stream uses its own. Like: Numbered pigeonholes in an office: mail for video goes in one slot, audio in another.

00:00:16.265 --> 00:00:19.505
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:00:20.555 --> 00:00:29.610
One USB cable carries live video, two-way sound, button presses and, sometimes, a firmware update, all at the same time.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:36.536
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:39.584
How does USB keep them from tripping over each other?

00:00:39.834 --> 00:00:42.969
With different transfer types and separate mailboxes.

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:49.047
Even a firmware update can share the cable during a call without hurting the video. Let's see how.

00:00:50.097 --> 00:00:52.326
USB is a strict classroom.

00:00:52.726 --> 00:00:56.209
The host is the teacher. No student speaks unless asked.

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:00.115
Each device has numbered mailboxes, called endpoints.

00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:03.615
Control transfers are paperwork, like registration.

00:01:04.015 --> 00:01:06.778
Bulk transfers are big parcels that can wait.

00:01:07.178 --> 00:01:14.562
Bulk transfer: A USB transfer type that is reliable but has no timing promise, used for files and updates. Like: Parcel post: everything arrives intact, but not at a promised minute.

00:01:14.812 --> 00:01:19.259
Interrupt transfers are small urgent notes, checked on a fixed schedule.

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:24.257
IRQ / interrupt: Interrupt request: a hardware signal that makes the processor stop and handle an event right now. Like: A doorbell: you stop what you are doing and answer it.

00:01:24.557 --> 00:01:31.174
And isochronous transfers are a live radio broadcast: if a piece arrives late, it's as useless as lost.

00:01:31.574 --> 00:01:38.343
Isochronous transfer: A USB transfer type with guaranteed timing but no retries, used for live video and audio. Like: A live TV broadcast: always on time, and a dropped frame is simply skipped, never resent.

00:01:39.443 --> 00:01:40.139
Words.

00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:48.190
An endpoint is a numbered pipe, either IN toward the host, or OUT from it. Endpoint zero is always the control endpoint.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.398
An interface is one function of the device, like video streaming.

00:01:52.798 --> 00:01:59.312
An alternate setting is a variant of an interface. Alt zero reserves no bandwidth; higher alts reserve more.

00:01:59.712 --> 00:02:06.376
A microframe is a one hundred twenty five microsecond time slot in high speed USB. There are eight per millisecond.

00:02:06.776 --> 00:02:14.090
And w Max Packet Size encodes the bytes per transaction, plus how many extra transactions fit in each microframe.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:15.905
Speeds.

00:02:16.305 --> 00:02:20.497
Low speed is one and a half megabits, full speed is twelve.

00:02:20.897 --> 00:02:24.472
High speed, the fastest USB two, is four hundred eighty.

00:02:24.872 --> 00:02:28.065
SuperSpeed, USB three, is five gigabits.

00:02:28.465 --> 00:02:30.822
SuperSpeed plus is ten or twenty.

00:02:31.222 --> 00:02:35.785
USB three adds separate wires, and companion descriptors for bursts.

00:02:36.985 --> 00:02:38.982
Let's watch one microframe.

00:02:39.382 --> 00:02:44.513
The host asks the video endpoint for data, and receives up to ten twenty four bytes.

00:02:44.913 --> 00:02:50.115
Because this endpoint declared a multiplier, the host asks again in the same microframe.

00:02:50.515 --> 00:02:54.717
And a third time. That's three transactions, reserved every microframe.

00:02:55.117 --> 00:03:02.037
Next, the button endpoint's polling interval is due. The device sends a report, or says NAK: nothing new.

00:03:02.437 --> 00:03:06.918
Bulk gets whatever time is left, which is why bulk has no timing guarantee.

00:03:07.318 --> 00:03:09.269
And the next microframe starts.

00:03:10.469 --> 00:03:13.116
Now the math that decides your camera's formats.

00:03:13.516 --> 00:03:20.389
This endpoint's packet size field is hex fourteen hundred. The low eleven bits are hex four hundred: ten twenty four bytes.

00:03:20.789 --> 00:03:25.445
Bits eleven and twelve are two, meaning three transactions per microframe.

00:03:25.845 --> 00:03:36.839
Ten twenty four, times three, times eight thousand microframes per second, is about twenty four and a half megabytes per second. That's the maximum for one high speed isochronous endpoint.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:43.694
Uncompressed ten eighty p thirty in YUY2 needs one hundred twenty four megabytes per second. It doesn't fit.

00:03:44.094 --> 00:03:51.699
YUY2 / NV12 / MJPEG: Ways to pack image pixels: brightness and colour stored separately (YUY2, NV12) or compressed per frame (MJPEG). Like: Different ways to pack a suitcase: rolled, folded, or vacuum-sealed.

00:03:51.949 --> 00:03:59.437
Compressed M J P E G does fit. That's why USB two webcams send M J P E G at ten eighty p.

00:03:59.837 --> 00:04:03.448
And even seven twenty p uncompressed needs USB three.

00:04:04.648 --> 00:04:10.674
Question for you: Live video uses a transfer type with no retries. Why is that the right choice?

00:04:15.974 --> 00:04:24.182
Answer: For live video, a late frame is useless. It's better to skip a damaged packet and keep the timing than to stop and resend.

00:04:25.282 --> 00:04:25.979
Recap.

00:04:26.379 --> 00:04:28.805
The host asks, the device answers.

00:04:29.205 --> 00:04:33.477
Four transfer types: control, bulk, interrupt, isochronous.

00:04:33.877 --> 00:04:37.732
Alt zero reserves no bandwidth; higher alts reserve it.

00:04:38.132 --> 00:04:43.565
One high speed isochronous endpoint tops out near twenty four and a half megabytes per second.

00:04:43.965 --> 00:04:48.052
And lab four parses these descriptors, including the multiplier bits.
